title: Storm-0940 / ConnectWise
page: breaches/storm-0940-connectwise.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: Microsoft assesses that Storm-0940 was behind the ConnectWise Cloud Backup compromise and used ARTEX agents to attempt sign-ins to 2,250+ tenants; it says most of the targeting failed.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 2,250+ | tenants where sign-in was attempted | a count of attempts, not successes, per Microsoft
stat: <3h | initial targeting to privileged access | in multiple instances, per Microsoft
stat: 7 | application identities disabled | by ConnectWise on October 17
Microsoft assesses that Storm-0940, a China-state aligned actor active since 2021, was responsible. How the credentials were taken has not been made public.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

The first is a count of attempts. Microsoft says ARTEX agents attempted sign-ins to more than 2,250 tenants, and that most of the targeting failed. It gives no number for tenants reached. It also says the group achieved privileged cloud persistence at many organizations in a brief time.

The second is speed, shown on the tile as less than three hours. In multiple instances, Microsoft says, initial targeting to privileged access took less than three hours.

The third is ConnectWise's response: it disabled seven application identities on October 17.

Microsoft assesses that Storm-0940, a China-state aligned actor active since 2021, was responsible. How the credentials were taken has not been made public. ConnectWise's advisory, last updated November 4, says the root cause is still under investigation.

Keep the attribution with each figure when you say it aloud.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: As early as September 2026 | Storm-0940's ARTEX activity, per Microsoft
event: October 10 to 14 | A spray that failed
event: October 15 | Public reports of a ConnectWise compromise begin
event: October 17 | ConnectWise disables seven app identities
event: October 28 | Phased restoration of Microsoft 365 backups begins
On November 4, ConnectWise said the root cause was still under investigation. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

Microsoft dates Storm-0940's ARTEX-driven activity to as early as September 2026. From October 10 to 14, a password spray hit 327 accounts at eight organizations, and Microsoft observed no success from it. Stage 02 covers how the page links the spray to the ConnectWise activity: a shared address.

Public reports of a compromise at ConnectWise Cloud Backup, formerly SkyKick, began on October 15. The next day ConnectWise reported investigating attempted unauthorized access, and it later described access to application identities and credentials. It disabled seven application identities on October 17 and finished resetting accounts and credentials on October 19.

Phased restoration of Microsoft 365 backups began on October 28. By November 4, ConnectWise reported every service restored except billing, notifications and some elements of the platform backup dashboard, and its advisory still said the root cause was under investigation. It says backup archives show no indication of compromise, and that each tenant still needs its own review.

== Who reported what
layout: table
row: Source | What the page draws from it
row: Microsoft | The assessment that Storm-0940 was responsible; sign-in attempts at 2,250+ tenants; the techniques
row: ConnectWise | Its advisory: root cause still under investigation as of November 4
row: Wiz | Overlapping observations: Okta, Entra ID and AWS Cognito targeting
row: GTIG, citing Wiz | UNC7228, which overlaps Storm-0940, from at least August 2026
Disclosure: the page cites research published by Wiz, and a Google Threat Intelligence report that draws on it. Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz.
notes:
The page draws on several sources, and each part of the story belongs to one of them.

Microsoft supplies the assessment that Storm-0940 was responsible, the count of more than 2,250 tenants where sign-in was attempted, and the techniques it observed. ConnectWise's advisory describes unauthorized access to application credentials associated with Cloud Backup, and says the root cause remains under investigation.

Wiz, whose observations overlap with Microsoft's Storm-0940 report, describes identity targeting on Okta, Entra ID and AWS Cognito. Google Threat Intelligence Group, GTIG for short, reports on a campaign Wiz tracks, which GTIG calls UNC7228, and says it overlaps with Storm-0940. Censys, at stage 01, describes where ARTEX came from.

Say the disclosure aloud as the page gives it. The page cites research published by Wiz, and a Google Threat Intelligence report that draws on it. Google acquired Wiz in March 2026, and the site's author works at Wiz. Every source is linked so you can read it directly.

== GTIG says UNC7228 overlaps Storm-0940, without calling them one actor
layout: compare
col: Storm-0940, per Microsoft | A China-state aligned actor, active since 2021 | ARTEX-driven activity as early as September 2026 | Initial targeting to privileged access in under 3 hours, in multiple instances
col: UNC7228, per GTIG citing Wiz | A campaign Wiz tracks, which GTIG says overlaps Storm-0940 | At least August 2026 | Typically around four hours from first compromised account to deep system access
The page keeps Microsoft's September date for Storm-0940. The two times start from different points, so they are not directly comparable.
notes:
Two names appear for activity that overlaps, and the page keeps them apart.

Microsoft dates Storm-0940's ARTEX-driven activity to as early as September 2026. GTIG says a campaign Wiz tracks, which GTIG calls UNC7228, overlaps with Storm-0940 and now dates back to at least August 2026. It reports that Wiz identified targeting, compromise and data theft at multiple entities in the US and Europe, in enterprise software, industrial automation, semiconductors and telecommunications.

GTIG describes the two as overlapping without saying they are one actor. So the page keeps Microsoft's September date for Storm-0940 itself, and this deck does the same.

Treat the two speeds with the same care. Microsoft reports multiple instances where initial targeting to privileged access took less than three hours. GTIG reports that the overlapping actor Wiz tracks typically moved from its first compromised account to deep system access in around four hours, and in one case in 15 minutes. Microsoft times its figure from initial targeting and GTIG from the first compromised account, so the two are not directly comparable.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Resource Development | An off-the-shelf agent framework | T1588.007
step: 02 | Credential Access | A spray that failed | T1110.003
step: 03 | Initial Access | The vendor's app identities | T1199
step: 04 | Lateral Movement | Thousands of tenants tried | T1078.004
step: 05 | Discovery | Microsoft Graph | T1087.004
step: 06 | Persistence | Credentials on existing apps | T1098.001
step: 07 | Privilege Escalation | Global Administrator | T1098.003
step: 08 | Credential Access | Key Vault at scale | T1555.006
Arrows show stage order, not cause; Microsoft saw no success from the spray.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's eight stages, each with its phase and the technique named in the stage's heading on the page.

Stage 01 is the agent framework Storm-0940 was running. Stage 02 is a password spray from which Microsoft observed no success. Stage 03 is initial access through ConnectWise's application identities, and stage 04 is the sign-in attempts at more than 2,250 tenants. In some tenants the agents went on to Microsoft Graph reconnaissance in stage 05 and added application credentials in stage 06. In stage 07 a ConnectWise management application assigned itself Global Administrator, and in stage 08 secrets were read across Key Vaults.

Read the arrows as stage order, not cause. Microsoft saw no success from the spray, and how the credentials were taken has not been made public. The link the page draws between the spray and the ConnectWise activity is a shared address, covered at stage 02.

The page does not trace one tenant through stages 04 to 08. It reports that activity across multiple customer environments.

== Storm-0940 was running ARTEX as early as September, per Microsoft
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1588.007 | Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence
fact: Framework | ARTEX: multi-agent, Go with a Next.js front end; any model
fact: State | assets and vulnerabilities stored in a graph
fact: Per Censys | published on GitHub as open source on July 26, 2026
fact: Availability | original repository taken down; forks and rebrands circulate
Microsoft says multiple actors in different regions use ARTEX.
notes:
Stage 01 covers the tool. The page maps it to T1588.007, Obtain Capabilities: Artificial Intelligence.

ARTEX is a multi-agent LLM system, LLM meaning large language model, written in Go with a Next.js front end. Its agents run an autonomous test from reconnaissance through data exfiltration, store discovered assets and vulnerabilities in a graph, and can use any AI model. Censys describes it as an agent layer on top of ScopeSentry, an asset-discovery and vulnerability-scanning platform by the same developer, and says ARTEX was published on GitHub as an open source project on July 26, 2026.

On October 8, the day after CrowdStrike reported ARTEX's use against South Korean finance, the developer denied involvement and said the project would go closed source. The original repository has since been taken down, and Censys and GTIG both report that forks and rebrands such as ScopeWeaver still circulate.

Microsoft describes Storm-0940 as an actor focused on data theft that historically relies on password spraying and internet-facing exploits, then OAuth application abuse for persistence. The pattern slide after the stages comes back to that.

== Named ARTEX users, and who named them
layout: table
row: Actor | Named by | What the page says
row: Storm-0940 | Microsoft | China-state aligned; ARTEX-driven activity as early as September 2026
row: VAPOR PANDA | CrowdStrike | China-nexus; it has its own csoh.org kill chain
row: Storm-3358 | Microsoft, November 6 | Cambodia-based, financially motivated; primarily targets financial organizations in Southeast Asia
row: A range of actors | GTIG | From extortion groups to suspected espionage operators, since late August 2026
Microsoft says multiple actors in different regions use ARTEX. Its Storm-3358 report names no victims and gives no count of intrusions.
notes:
Microsoft says multiple actors in different regions use ARTEX, and other users have since been named. Each name comes from its own source.

CrowdStrike reports the China-nexus VAPOR PANDA using ARTEX. The page lists it as another user of the framework and links its kill chain.

Microsoft's November 6 report names Storm-3358, which it describes as a financially motivated, Cambodia-based intrusion cluster that primarily targets financial organizations in Southeast Asia. Microsoft says Storm-3358 began using ARTEX against the financial sector in October 2026, exploiting flaws including anonymous file upload and server-side request forgery, and installing the WISP, vshell and AdaptixC2 remote access trojans. That report names no victims and gives no count of intrusions.

GTIG says that since late August 2026, ARTEX has been adopted by actors ranging from financially motivated extortion groups to suspected espionage operators.

== A spray on 327 accounts at eight organizations; Microsoft saw no success
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1110.003 | Brute Force: Password Spraying
fact: Target | Microsoft 365 and Exchange Online, October 10 to 14
fact: Volume | 151,000+ failed logons; peak 2,900 a minute on October 11
fact: Overlap, per Wiz | Okta, Entra ID and AWS Cognito targeting; most attempts failed
Its address, 144.172.64.99, appears in ConnectWise's indicators, which helps tie the spray to the ConnectWise activity.
notes:
From October 10 to 14, infrastructure at 144.172.64.99 sprayed 327 accounts across eight organizations, aimed at Microsoft 365 and Exchange Online. The page maps this to T1110.003, Password Spraying.

Microsoft counted more than 151,000 failed logons, over 111,000 of them on the peak day, reaching 2,900 failures per minute on October 11. 48 accounts took more than 1,000 failures each. That rate belongs to this spray and describes failed logons only. Microsoft observed no successful authentication, token issuance, Graph call or directory change from it.

The spray's value to investigators came afterwards: the same address appears in ConnectWise's indicators, which helps tie the two activities together.

Wiz, whose observations overlap with Microsoft's Storm-0940 report, lists 144.172.64.60, .99 and .128 as addresses Microsoft attributes to Storm-0940. It describes the actor it tracked targeting multiple IT and software vendors throughout October. Its spraying hit Okta and Entra ID, targeting hundreds of users, mainly software engineers, with a median of one second between attempts. On AWS Cognito it repeatedly called sign-in, password-reset, confirmation-code and sign-up APIs. Wiz says most of these attempts failed. The identity targeting it describes reaches beyond Microsoft 365.

== What password spraying is
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1110.003 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1110/003/
Password spraying tries one password, or a short list of common ones, against many accounts. Spreading the attempts across accounts avoids the lockouts that many guesses against one account would trigger.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes password spraying as using a single password, or a small list of commonly used passwords that may match the domain's complexity policy, against many different accounts. Logins are attempted that way to avoid the account lockouts that would normally occur when one account is brute forced with many passwords.

Besides management services such as SSH, RDP and LDAP, ATT&CK says adversaries may target single sign-on and cloud-based applications that use federated authentication protocols, as well as externally facing email applications such as Office 365.

It also notes that adversaries may deliberately throttle password spraying attempts to avoid triggering security alerting.

== Control of ConnectWise app identities opened a trusted route into tenants
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
technique: T1195 | Supply Chain Compromise
fact: Product | ConnectWise Cloud Backup, formerly SkyKick
fact: Named by Microsoft | Cloud Backup, MS Teams and MS Graph applications, among others
fact: Entry vector | not published as of ConnectWise's November 4 update
fact: App-only tokens | need no user, so no interactive sign-in and no MFA prompt
A backup product needs broad access, which stays in place between backups.
notes:
ConnectWise describes unauthorized access to application credentials associated with Cloud Backup. In its October 20 update it was still working with outside specialists to learn how that access first happened. Its advisory, last updated November 4, still says the root cause, including how access to those credentials was obtained, remains under investigation. Microsoft says the activity did not come from a compromise of, or a vulnerability in, its corporate systems.

The page maps this stage to T1199, Trusted Relationship, and tags T1195, Supply Chain Compromise.

A multi-tenant application is one identity consented into many tenants. Whoever holds its credential can request a token for any tenant that granted consent, with no user present. That is what makes a backup vendor an attractive target: the product needs broad access to do its job, and that access stays in place between backups. MFA is multi-factor authentication, and an app-only token raises no MFA prompt.

Microsoft has since described a separate campaign it links to ARTEX, in which a vendor-owned multitenant application credential was used to reach downstream tenants. Todyl assesses that activity as unrelated to the ConnectWise compromise. It has its own kill chain, linked at the end of this deck.

The next slide explains app-only access in general terms.

== Delegated access and app-only access
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, permissions and consent overview | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/permissions-consent-overview
An app can act for a signed-in user, or as itself with no user. Microsoft lists automation and backup among app-only uses. Application permissions reach any data the permission covers, and generally only an administrator can consent to them.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

In the Microsoft identity platform, an application reaches protected data in one of two ways. With delegated access, a user has signed in and the app acts on that user's behalf, so it can reach nothing the user could not. With app-only access, the app acts as its own identity with no user signed in. Microsoft lists scenarios such as automation and backup, and apps that run as background services or daemons.

App-only access uses application permissions, also called app roles. An app granted an application permission can access any data that permission is associated with. Microsoft's example: an app granted Microsoft Graph's Files.Read.All application permission can read any file in the tenant through Microsoft Graph.

Consent is how users or administrators authorize an app to reach a protected resource. In general, only an administrator or the owner of an API's service principal can consent to the application permissions that API exposes.

== ARTEX agents attempted sign-in to 2,250+ tenants that trusted the apps
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Attempted | more than 2,250 tenants
fact: Succeeded | not published; multiple customer environments saw follow-on activity
fact: Mechanism | existing application trust, no interactive sign-in
Even a successful authentication does not necessarily mean customer data was accessed or taken, Microsoft warns.
notes:
With the application credentials in hand, the agents authenticated to customer tenants, relying on the trust each application already held there. The page maps this to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts, under its Lateral Movement phase.

Read the figure carefully. More than 2,250 is Microsoft's count of tenants where sign-in was attempted. Microsoft says most of the targeting was not successful, and it gives no number for tenants reached. It reports multiple customer environments with follow-on activity, which the next stages describe.

Microsoft also warns against reading too much into a single indicator. The presence of an affected application, or even a successful authentication, does not necessarily mean customer data was accessed or taken.

If someone asks how many tenants were compromised, the answer from the page is that the number has not been published.

== In some tenants, agents moved on to Microsoft Graph and directory reconnaissance
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Discovery
technique: T1087.004 | Account Discovery: Cloud Account
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
fact: Interface | Microsoft Graph and directory operations
fact: Microsoft's term | AI-accelerated reconnaissance
fact: Log source | MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs alongside SigninLogs
Agents can work around the clock, which shortens the distance from picking a target to reaching an objective.
notes:
In some environments the agents went past authentication into Microsoft Graph and directory operations. Microsoft calls this AI-accelerated reconnaissance. The page maps it to T1087.004, Account Discovery: Cloud Account, and the mapping in Microsoft's report also lists T1580, Cloud Infrastructure Discovery.

The page adds a point about time. Agents can work around the clock, which shortens the distance from picking a target to reaching an objective.

For defenders, the page names the log source: MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs alongside SigninLogs. The defense slides come back to reading sign-ins and Graph activity together.

The next slide explains Microsoft Graph in general terms.

== What Microsoft Graph is
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, overview of Microsoft Graph | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/overview
Microsoft Graph is one API, at graph.microsoft.com, for data in Microsoft cloud services such as Microsoft Entra and Microsoft 365, from users and groups to mail, files and Teams. Only authorized callers can reach the data.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Microsoft describes Graph as the gateway to data and intelligence in Microsoft cloud services like Microsoft Entra and Microsoft 365. The Microsoft Graph API offers a single endpoint, graph.microsoft.com, reached through REST APIs or SDKs.

Through it, apps reach Microsoft 365 core services such as Outlook and Exchange, OneDrive, SharePoint, Teams and Calendar, and Enterprise Mobility and Security services including Microsoft Entra and Intune. Microsoft's own example shows the directory side: look up a user in Microsoft Entra ID, follow the manager relationship, then reach the user's groups and the other members of each group.

Microsoft Graph is secured, and only authorized callers can access the data.

== Added credentials created a way back that could outlive the vendor's cleanup
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Persistence
technique: T1098.001 | Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Credentials
fact: Technique | add or modify credentials on existing OAuth applications
fact: Microsoft warns | a backdoor that can survive remediation of the original app
fact: Where to look | Entra audit records: credential additions, new owners, consent grants
ConnectWise rotating its own credentials says nothing about a credential the attacker added inside your tenant.
notes:
Storm-0940 added or modified credentials on existing OAuth applications to keep its access to Microsoft 365. The page maps this to T1098.001, Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Credentials. OAuth is an authorization framework that issues access tokens.

Microsoft warns that such changes can leave a backdoor through other identities, credentials and cloud services, and that it can survive after the original application is remediated.

This is the point to stress. ConnectWise rotating its own credentials says nothing about a credential the attacker added inside your tenant. ConnectWise itself says each tenant still needs its own review, and the page says where to look: Entra audit records, for credential additions, new owners and consent grants.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what adding credentials to an application gives an attacker.

== What ATT&CK calls Additional Cloud Credentials
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1098.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1098/001/
An adversary adds credentials it controls to a cloud account, in addition to the legitimate ones, to keep persistent access. In Entra ID these include x509 keys and passwords on applications and service principals.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes adversaries adding adversary-controlled credentials to a cloud account to maintain persistent access to victim accounts and instances. In Azure and Entra ID, its example is adding credentials for service principals and applications in addition to existing legitimate credentials, and it says these include both x509 keys and passwords. With sufficient permissions, they can be added through the Azure portal, the Azure command line interface, or PowerShell modules.

ATT&CK lists the technique under both Persistence and Privilege Escalation.

== One ConnectWise management application assigned itself Global Administrator
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Privilege Escalation
technique: T1098.003 | Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Roles
fact: Observed | an application added itself to a high-privilege role
fact: Speed | under 3 hours from targeting to privileged access, in multiple instances, per Microsoft
fact: Defender XDR alert | Application self-assigned Global Administrator role
An application able to grant itself a directory role was already holding permissions close to that level.
notes:
Microsoft lists self-assignment of Global Administrator by a ConnectWise management application among the observed techniques, and describes changes to identities, groups and privileged roles. The page maps this to T1098.003, Account Manipulation: Additional Cloud Roles.

Microsoft's three-hour figure ends at this step. It reports multiple instances where initial targeting to privileged access took less than three hours, against a campaign in which most of the targeting failed.

GTIG reports that the overlapping actor Wiz tracks typically moved from its first compromised account to deep system access in around four hours, and in one case in 15 minutes. Microsoft times its figure from initial targeting and GTIG from the first compromised account, so the two are not directly comparable.

The page lists a Microsoft Defender XDR alert for this step: Application self-assigned Global Administrator role. XDR stands for extended detection and response.

== What the Global Administrator role can do
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Microsoft Entra built-in roles | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/role-based-access-control/permissions-reference
Global Administrator can manage all aspects of Microsoft Entra ID and the Microsoft services that use Entra identities. Microsoft marks it a privileged role, and its holders can reset any user's or administrator's password.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Microsoft marks Global Administrator as a privileged role. It gives access to all administrative features in Microsoft Entra ID, and to services that use Microsoft Entra identities, such as the Microsoft Defender portal, the Microsoft Purview portal, Exchange Online and SharePoint Online.

Global Administrators can reset the password for any user and all other administrators.

== Secrets were read at scale across multiple Azure Key Vaults
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1555.006 | Credentials from Password Stores: Cloud Secrets Management Stores
technique: T1528 | Steal Application Access Token
fact: Bulk secret reads | across multiple Key Vaults, after cloud resource enumeration
fact: Also | key enumeration and a key-unwrap operation
fact: Backup archives | no indication of compromise, per ConnectWise
Each secret taken extends the incident beyond Microsoft 365, into what it unlocks.
notes:
Microsoft reports cloud resource enumeration followed by bulk secret reads across multiple Azure Key Vaults, along with key enumeration and a key-unwrap operation. The page maps this to T1555.006, Credentials from Password Stores: Cloud Secrets Management Stores, and also tags T1528, Steal Application Access Token.

Key Vault is Azure's store for keys, secrets and certificates. Each secret taken from a vault extends the incident beyond Microsoft 365, into whatever those secrets unlock.

ConnectWise, for its part, says backup archives remain encrypted, isolated and preserved with no indication of compromise.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what a key-unwrap operation is.

== What a Key Vault key unwrap does
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Key Vault key types, algorithms and operations | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/keys/about-keys-details
credit: Also from the Key Vault REST API reference, Unwrap Key (learn.microsoft.com)
A key held in Key Vault can protect another key, typically a symmetric content encryption key. Wrapping protects that key, and unwrapping uses the same vault key to decrypt it. The vault does not export key material.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Microsoft says Key Vault does not support export operations: once a key is provisioned, its key material cannot be extracted.

One of the operations a stored key supports is key encryption, or wrapping. A key in the service protects another key, typically a symmetric content encryption key. The wrapKey permission lets a caller use the key to protect a symmetric key, and unwrapKey lets it use the key to unprotect a wrapped one. Microsoft says keeping wrap and unwrap as distinct operations, rather than reusing encrypt and decrypt, gives semantic and authorization separation.

The REST API reference describes unwrap as the reverse of wrap: it decrypts a symmetric key using the key that was first used to wrap it, and it requires the keys/unwrapKey permission.

Also from the Key Vault REST API reference, Unwrap Key: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/keyvault/keys/unwrap-key/unwrap-key

== Storm-0940's usual methods are not new; the operator is what changed
layout: text
Microsoft says Storm-0940 historically relies on password spraying, internet-facing exploits and OAuth app abuse. None of that is new.
- In multiple instances, targeting to privileged access took under 3 hours, per Microsoft.
- Microsoft also says most of the targeting failed.
notes:
The page draws this pattern at stage 01, and comes back to speed at stages 05 and 07.

Microsoft describes Storm-0940 as an actor focused on data theft that historically relies on password spraying and internet-facing exploits, then OAuth application abuse for persistence. None of that is new. The operator is what changed.

Agents can work around the clock, which shortens the distance from picking a target to reaching an objective. Microsoft reports multiple instances where initial targeting to privileged access took less than three hours.

Keep the rest of Microsoft's account beside the speed. Microsoft says most of the targeting failed, and also that the group achieved privileged cloud persistence at many organizations in a brief time.

== Six defenses against eight stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07 | 08
row: Inventory multi-tenant apps by application ID | 03 | 07
row: Preserve evidence before deleting anything | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Alert on app credential and role changes | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Shrink what third-party apps can do | 03 | 07
row: Watch vault access per identity | 08
row: Confirm the vendor's fix covers your tenant | 03 | 06
No defense on the page is tied by its words to stage 01 or 02.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the eight stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words name something that stage reports.

Inventorying multi-tenant applications answers stage 03's multi-tenant route, and asks whether any application could grant itself more, as one did in stage 07. Preserving evidence keeps the sign-in records and Graph telemetry stage 05 names as its log source, reviews the application sign-ins stage 04 reports, and keeps the Entra audit records where stage 06 says to look. Alerting covers stage 06's credential additions, new owners and consent grants, stage 07's role assignment, and, through service principal sign-ins with Graph activity, stages 04 and 05. Shrinking third-party access answers stage 03's multi-tenant permissions and, through its review of high-privilege applications, stage 07's application that added itself to a high-privilege role. Watching vault access answers stage 08's bulk reads, enumeration and unwrap. Confirming the vendor's fix checks that rotating the credentials stage 03 reports accessed removed any remaining access, and answers stage 06's warning that the vendor's rotation says nothing about a credential added inside your tenant.

No defense is tied by its words to stage 01, the framework, or stage 02, the failed spray. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Inventory apps by ID, and preserve evidence before deleting
layout: compare
col: Inventory by application ID | Search every tenant that installed or authorized the apps, even ones no longer used | Display names can mislead | ConnectWise's advisory lists seven app IDs and three suspicious addresses | Ask of every vendor: could any app grant itself more?
col: Preserve evidence first | Keep affected connections disabled; don't delete enterprise apps first, per Microsoft | Keep Entra sign-in and audit records, Purview audit logs, Graph telemetry | Microsoft: review from at least October 15; service principal sign-ins from October 1 | ConnectWise: review from October 13
notes:
The first two defenses come before any cleanup.

Inventory multi-tenant applications by application ID. Search every tenant where the applications were installed or authorized, including ones no longer used, because display names can mislead. ConnectWise's advisory lists seven application IDs and three suspicious addresses. Then ask the wider question for every vendor: which applications hold standing permissions here, and could any of them grant themselves more?

Preserve evidence before you delete anything. Microsoft's advice is to keep affected connections disabled and not to delete enterprise applications as a first response. Preserve Entra sign-in and audit records, Purview audit logs and Graph telemetry. Microsoft says to review from at least October 15 and to extend service principal sign-in review to October 1, and ConnectWise says to review from October 13. Deleting the application destroys context you will need.

ConnectWise's FAQ says its old application can be deleted safely once the tenant is re-authenticated with the new one and any necessary logs have been gathered.

== Alert on app credential and role changes, and shrink third-party access
layout: compare
col: Alert on app changes | Credential additions, new owners, app-role and privileged role assignments, consent grants: all rare | An app adding itself to a directory role deserves an immediate page | Review service principal sign-ins with Graph activity
col: Shrink third-party access | Least privilege for multi-tenant permissions and consent grants | Ask vendors to justify directory write access; review high-privilege apps | Workload identity Conditional Access does not cover a vendor's multi-tenant app
For those apps, the page says, your controls are consent scope and monitoring.
notes:
Alert on changes to application credentials and roles. Credential additions, new owners, app-role assignments, consent grants and privileged role assignments are all rare events, and all of them appear in this chain. An application adding itself to a directory role deserves an immediate page. Review service principal sign-ins together with Graph activity, since neither shows the whole picture alone.

Shrink what third-party applications can do. Apply least privilege to multi-tenant permissions and consent grants, and ask vendors to justify directory write access. Review high-privilege applications on a schedule, and remove consent for products you stopped using.

Know the limit of Conditional Access here. Conditional Access for workload identities can bind single-tenant principals you own to known networks, but it does not cover a vendor's multi-tenant application. For those, the page says, your controls are consent scope and monitoring.

== Watch vault access per identity, and confirm the vendor's fix
layout: compare
col: Watch vault access | Bulk reads across several vaults by one app: a clear signal, given a baseline | Give each app its own vault, as Microsoft recommends | Alert on unusual enumeration and unwrap operations
col: Confirm the vendor's fix | Check that credential rotation removed remaining unauthorized access | Assess what the outage did to backup and restore | Re-authorization alone does not confirm backups resumed; verify new snapshots | Don't re-enable disabled apps unless ConnectWise says to
notes:
Watch vault access per identity. Bulk reads across several vaults by one application is a clear signal when each identity has a baseline. Give each application its own vault, as Microsoft recommends, so that one compromised identity cannot read the rest, and alert on unusual enumeration and unwrap operations.

For a separate campaign, Microsoft also suggests hunting for one application requesting tokens for Azure Resource Manager, Key Vault, Storage and Graph in a short window. Keep that attribution when you present it.

Confirm the vendor's fix covers your tenant. Check that credential rotation removed any remaining unauthorized access, and assess what the outage did to backup and restore. Re-authorization alone does not confirm backups have resumed, so verify that new snapshots complete. ConnectWise's November 4 update repeats that point, and says not to re-enable previously disabled applications unless ConnectWise instructs you to.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can you list every multi-tenant app by application ID, including ones no longer used?
check: Could any vendor app in your tenant grant itself more?
check: Would you keep sign-in, audit and Graph records before deleting an enterprise app?
check: Would an app adding itself to a directory role page someone immediately?
check: Can each vendor justify the directory write access its app holds?
check: Does each app have its own Key Vault, with a baseline for its reads?
check: After a vendor rotates its credentials, would you find one added inside your tenant?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether you could list every multi-tenant application by application ID, including ones no longer used, and whether any vendor application could grant itself more. Ask whether your response plan keeps Entra sign-in and audit records, Purview audit logs and Graph telemetry before anyone deletes an enterprise application.

Ask whether an application adding itself to a directory role would page someone immediately, and whether each vendor can justify the directory write access its application holds.

Ask whether each application has its own vault, with a baseline that would make bulk reads stand out. Finally, ask how you would find a credential an attacker added inside your tenant after the vendor rotated its own.

== The vendor rotated its credentials. Is your tenant clean?
layout: question
ConnectWise disabled seven application identities on October 17 and finished resetting accounts and credentials on October 19.

What would you check in your own tenant before calling it clean?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The vendor's side of the response is on the timeline: seven application identities disabled on October 17, accounts and credentials reset by October 19, and phased restoration of Microsoft 365 backups from October 28.

Ask each person to name the first record they would open, and what they would look for in it. The answer slide gives what the page says.

== The vendor's rotation says nothing about credentials added in your tenant
layout: answer
ConnectWise says each tenant still needs its own review.
- Search Entra audit records for credential additions, new owners, consent grants.
- Check that credential rotation removed any remaining unauthorized access.
- Verify new backup snapshots complete; re-authorization alone does not confirm it.
notes:
The page's answer is that the vendor's rotation does not settle it.

Storm-0940 added or modified credentials on existing OAuth applications, and Microsoft warns that such a backdoor can survive after the original application is remediated. ConnectWise rotating its own credentials says nothing about a credential the attacker added inside your tenant, and ConnectWise itself says each tenant still needs its own review.

Start in the Entra audit records: credential additions, new owners and consent grants. Check that the vendor's credential rotation removed any remaining unauthorized access. Then assess what the outage did to backup and restore, because re-authorization alone does not confirm backups have resumed. Verify that new snapshots complete.

ConnectWise's November 4 update also says not to re-enable previously disabled applications unless ConnectWise instructs you to.

== What would you add for stages 01 and 02?
layout: question
By their words, the page's defenses reach stages 03 to 08, not the framework or the failed spray.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

The page offers six defenses. By their own words they reach stages 03 to 08, from the multi-tenant application route to the Key Vault reads. None names stage 01, the ARTEX framework, or stage 02, the spray from which Microsoft observed no success.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stages 01 and 02
layout: answer
Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against reported details, like the spray from 144.172.64.99, or ARTEX's forks and rebrands.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. By the matrix's rule, no defense is tied to stage 01 or stage 02, and this deck adds no defense of its own.

Test each proposal against the facts the page reports. For stage 01, those are ARTEX itself, its forks and rebrands, and its other named users. For stage 02, they are the spray from 144.172.64.99 on October 10 to 14, its peak of 2,900 failures a minute, and Wiz's account of Okta, Entra ID and AWS Cognito targeting.

