notes:
These are primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

Microsoft's report covers Storm-0940 and ARTEX, and its earlier post gives background on Storm-0940. ConnectWise's Trust Center carries the Cloud Backup advisory. Microsoft's November 6 report covers Storm-3358. GTIG's report covers ARTEX and other agentic frameworks and draws on Wiz's research, and Wiz's post describes the overlapping activity it tracked; the site's author works at Wiz. Censys describes ARTEX's origins, and The Hacker News reported on the campaign.

The page also cites Todyl, Microsoft's post on agentic tooling and exposed secrets, and the MITRE pages for T1098.001 and T1555.006.

The background slides name their own sources, from MITRE ATT&CK and Microsoft Learn.

== Related kill chains and defense topics
layout: links
link: VAPOR PANDA / ARTEX | breaches/vapor-panda-artex.html
link: ARTEX exposed-secrets kill chain | breaches/artex-github-secrets-azure.html
link: IAM & cloud identity | iam.html
link: Azure security | azure-security.html
link: Detection engineering | detection-engineering.html
link: Incident response | incident-response.html
CrowdStrike reports VAPOR PANDA using ARTEX. Microsoft has since described a separate campaign it links to ARTEX; Todyl assesses it as unrelated to the ConnectWise compromise.
notes:
The page links two other kill chains, and four defense topics.

At stage 01, the page names VAPOR PANDA among ARTEX's other users, as CrowdStrike reports, and links its kill chain. The page lists it as another user of the framework.

At stage 03, the page notes a separate campaign Microsoft links to ARTEX, in which a vendor-owned multitenant application credential was used to reach downstream tenants. Todyl assesses that activity as unrelated to the ConnectWise compromise. It is the ARTEX exposed-secrets kill chain.

The four topics are the page's related defense topics: IAM and cloud identity, Azure security, detection engineering, and incident response. IAM stands for identity and access management.
